WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.665
This is the film for your camera.

2
00:00:03.415 --> 00:00:07.073
It's a strip of plastic, 35 millimetres wide.

3
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.215
Light touches it for about a three-hundredth of a second.

4
00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:13.985
Weeks later, the picture is still there.

5
00:00:13.735 --> 00:00:17.002
So how does a strip of plastic remember a picture?

6
00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:21.028
Most people guess that the light simply draws the picture,

7
00:00:20.778 --> 00:00:24.128
there and then, the way it seems to on a phone.

8
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:28.723
If that were true, you could open the back of the camera and look at it.

9
00:00:28.473 --> 00:00:31.293
And if you did, you'd see plain film.

10
00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:32.684
Nothing on it at all.

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.135
People did try using light on its own.

12
00:00:35.885 --> 00:00:42.006
By 1727, Johann Schulze had shown that silver salts go dark in light.

13
00:00:41.756 --> 00:00:48.466
Around 1800, Thomas Wedgwood coated paper with them and let the sun print shadows onto it.

14
00:00:48.216 --> 00:00:50.796
But inside a camera it failed.

15
00:00:50.546 --> 00:00:55.794
And out in daylight, his pictures just kept going, until they were dark all over.

16
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.080
So light alone has two problems.

17
00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:00.679
It's far too slow.

18
00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:01.370
And it never stops.

19
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.495
Film is much cleverer than that.

20
00:01:04.245 --> 00:01:11.295
On the plastic there's a coat of gelatin, which was Richard Maddox's idea in 1871.

21
00:01:11.045 --> 00:01:14.960
And sitting in the gelatin are crystals of silver salt,

22
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.060
each about a thousandth of a millimetre across.

23
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:24.010
When light strikes one of those crystals, a few atoms of silver gather into a tiny speck.

24
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.487
About four atoms is enough, which is far too few for anyone to see.

25
00:01:28.237 --> 00:01:30.812
But that crystal is now marked.

26
00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:33.841
Bright parts of the scene mark a lot of crystals.

27
00:01:33.591 --> 00:01:36.151
Dark parts mark only a few.

28
00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:38.586
And that invisible pattern is your picture.

29
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.675
Then, in the dark, the film goes into a bath called developer.

30
00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:48.910
Developer takes each marked crystal and turns the whole thing into black silver,

31
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.425
and it leaves the unmarked ones alone.

32
00:01:51.175 --> 00:01:53.804
A few atoms become billions.

33
00:01:53.554 --> 00:01:57.514
The light's work gets multiplied about a billion times.

34
00:01:57.264 --> 00:02:01.450
Daguerre's silver plates, made public in 1839,

35
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.479
worked like this, with mercury fumes bringing the picture out.

36
00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:11.061
So did Talbot's paper in 1841, where an hour's wait became a minute or two.

37
00:02:10.811 --> 00:02:15.866
There was one last problem: the unmarked crystals can still darken later.

38
00:02:15.616 --> 00:02:21.426
In 1839, John Herschel showed that a salt called hypo dissolves them away.

39
00:02:21.176 --> 00:02:22.771
That's fixing.

40
00:02:22.521 --> 00:02:28.062
Developed and fixed, the picture is plain silver, and light can't change it any more.

41
00:02:28.767 --> 00:02:33.937
Where the scene was brightest, there's the most silver, so the film is at its darkest.

42
00:02:33.687 --> 00:02:38.222
A white cloud comes out black, and a dark doorway comes out clear.

43
00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:39.542
It's a negative.

44
00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.052
Shine light through it onto fresh paper,

45
00:02:41.802 --> 00:02:45.822
and backwards gets turned backwards again: a positive.

46
00:02:45.572 --> 00:02:48.242
Herschel chose both of those words.

47
00:02:47.992 --> 00:02:52.171
And one Talbot negative could print as many positives as you liked.

48
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.485
Colour film is the same trick, done three times over.

49
00:02:56.235 --> 00:03:01.970
It's three films in one: one layer catches blue light, one green, one red.

50
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.332
In developing, each layer's silver makes a dye, and then the silver is washed out.

51
00:03:07.082 --> 00:03:12.847
What's left is three dye pictures, in backwards colours, under an orange tint.

52
00:03:12.597 --> 00:03:19.592
Layered colour film went on sale in 1935, and colour negatives for prints in 1942.

53
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.590
Now a sum.

54
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.105
We count light in steps, where one step is double, or half.

55
00:03:24.855 --> 00:03:29.860
Colour negative film forgives one step too little, and three steps too much.

56
00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.715
One step under is a half.

57
00:03:31.465 --> 00:03:35.704
Three steps over is two times two times two, which is eight.

58
00:03:35.454 --> 00:03:38.389
Eight divided by a half is sixteen.

59
00:03:38.139 --> 00:03:42.089
So the window you have to hit is sixteen times wide.

60
00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:47.030
And that is why your camera can get away with only one shutter speed and three holes.

61
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:50.118
The box tells you the film's speed.

62
00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:55.778
ISO 400 needs a quarter of the light that ISO 100 needs.

63
00:03:55.528 --> 00:04:02.265
With ISO 400, your camera can take a picture anywhere from full sun down to bright cloud.

64
00:04:02.967 --> 00:04:07.716
After each picture, you wind the film back into its shell, back into the dark.

65
00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:10.013
And keep the back shut.

66
00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:13.096
Until it's developed, film goes on catching light.

67
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.280
Posting film away to be developed is an old idea.

68
00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:22.411
In 1888, the first Kodak went back to its factory, camera and all.

69
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.273
So how does plastic remember?

70
00:04:25.023 --> 00:04:29.838
Light marks the crystals, and developing turns the marks into a picture.

71
00:04:29.588 --> 00:04:31.798
You can see it for yourself.

72
00:04:31.548 --> 00:04:36.937
Hold an old negative up to a window, and the darkest parts were the brightest things.

73
00:04:36.687 --> 00:04:40.773
And you can try it: leave a key on dark paper in the sun,

74
00:04:40.523 --> 00:04:43.552
and see just how slow light is on its own.

75
00:04:43.302 --> 00:04:45.773
Which brings us to the next question,

76
00:04:45.523 --> 00:04:50.997
because your camera has three holes to choose from, and their numbers look like nonsense.

77
00:04:50.747 --> 00:04:54.567
Why do camera numbers go eight, eleven, sixteen?

78
00:04:54.317 --> 00:04:57.772
Every picture here is drawn or simulated.

79
00:04:57.522 --> 00:05:00.595
No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.

